<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Funiculì, Funiculà</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Funiculì, funiculà</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Funiculì, Funiculà&quot; is a Neapolitan song composed in 1880 by Luigi Denza in his home town of Castellammare di Stabia, with words by the journalist Peppino Turco. It celebrated the opening that year of the first funicular railway on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a>, and it was Turco who prompted Denza to write it, perhaps as a joke.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was composed in 1880 by Luigi Denza in his hometown of Castellammare di Stabia, with lyrics by the journalist Peppino Turco, to commemorate the opening of the first funicular railway on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a>. It was first sung at the Quisisana Hotel in Castellammare di Stabia and was presented by Turco and Denza at the Piedigrotta festival the same year. The publisher Casa Ricordi sold over a million copies of the sheet music within a year, and the song became immensely popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and abroad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In Turco's Neapolitan lyrics a young man compares his sweetheart to a volcano and invites her to a romantic trip to the summit. The singer says that from the top one can see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Procida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and ends by hoping to marry her. The chorus repeats the word funiculì, funiculà, which gives the song its name. Edward Oxenford wrote English lyrics with scant relationship to the original, which became traditional in English-speaking countries and often appear under the title A Merry Life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some composers who borrowed the tune took it for a traditional Neapolitan folk song. Richard Strauss heard it on a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> six years after it was written and used it in his tone poem Aus Italien; Denza sued and won, and Strauss had to pay him a royalty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</a> likewise used it in his Neapolitan Song of 1907. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1884 the publishers Spear and Dehnhoff issued the melody as Tra-la-la-lee without crediting Denza. Arnold Schoenberg arranged a version for ensemble in 1921.</p><p>Denza died in 1922, and the song has been in the public domain since 1 January 1993. It has been recorded by many artists, among them Beniamino Gigli, Mario Lanza, the Mills Brothers, Connie Francis, Luciano Pavarotti and Andrea Bocelli, and sung in Japanese and German versions. It has been parodied and used in films, television, games and advertising, and in 1991 a parody, Pizza Song, probably helped associate it with pizza.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Funiculì, Funiculà” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Funiculì, Funiculà</b>&quot; is a Neapolitan song composed in 1880 by Luigi Denza in his home town of Castellammare di Stabia, with words by the journalist Peppino Turco.</blockquote><a href="/songs/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a></li><li><blockquote>It became one of the most popular songs of the Neapolitan tradition, beside &quot;'O sole mio&quot;, &quot;<b>Funiculì, Funiculà</b>&quot; and &quot;Santa Lucia&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a></li></ul></section>
